# Local preview matches production

When you change this site, the local version must work the same way as the server. The Vite dev server (`npm run dev -w frontend`, port 5173) is not the real site.

In production, the NestJS backend (`backend/`) runs on Node behind the Cloudflare proxy or a Tunnel. It serves the built React apps from `frontend/dist/public` and `frontend/dist/admin`. It also handles the SPA routes, the `/api` routes, the `/admin` gate and the cron, with SQLite (`DATABASE_PATH`). Vite on its own does none of the backend work: it does not gate admin, set the CSRF cookie or return 404 status codes.

## How to run it

- Preview with `npm run serve` (`scripts/dev.mjs`). It runs `vite build --watch` for both apps and `nest start --watch` on http://localhost:8080/, with the local SQLite file. Migrations are applied on start.
- `npm run serve -- --hmr` also starts Vite with HMR on :5173 (proxy `/api` → :8080), for quick markup work only.

## Routing that must keep working

- These public SPA routes return `frontend/dist/public/index.html` with status 200: `/`, `/raffles/`, `/leaderboards/`, `/leaderboards/<slug>/`, `/account/`, `/bonuses/`, `/cases/`. A path without the trailing slash gets a 301 to the slash version. Any other path returns index.html with status 404, and the SPA renders the not-found view.
- The browser URL stays `/leaderboards/<slug>/` and `/admin/leaderboards/<slug>/`. React Router reads the slug.
- Every `/admin*` path, `/admin/assets/*` included, passes the Cloudflare Access check first. Admin pages then return `frontend/dist/admin/index.html`.
- `/api/...` comes from NestJS and SQLite, never from static files. An unknown `/api` path answers a JSON 404.
- HTML responses carry the `kdeenoff_csrf` cookie and `cache-control: no-cache`.

## When you change the site

Verify the changed page through http://localhost:8080/ with `npm run serve` running. Click the header links that the change affects. `npm run typecheck` must pass. A page that only works under the Vite dev server is not done.
